   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general municipal law, in relation to requiring\nmunicipalities to maintain municipal websites",
    "basePrintNo": "S3353",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3353-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 34,
       "fullName": "Nathalia Fernandez",
       "imgName": "966_Nathalia_Fernandez.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1520,
       "sessionMemberId": 1883,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "FERNANDEZ"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 13-E §300, amd §30, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3353\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to requiring\nmunicipalities to maintain municipal websites\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would require all counties, cities, towns and villages to\nmaintain an official website.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends the general municipal law by adding a new article 13-e\nentitled \"municipal websites\".\n\nThe legislation will require municipalities to maintain a website that\nis accessible to the public and provides important information to resi-\ndents. websites must contain basic information, financial reports, meet-\ning and election notices, meeting agendas and minutes, and an updated\nversion of local code or laws.\n\nSection 2: amends section 30 of the general municipal law as it relates\nto clarifying that all municipalities must post financial reports on\ntheir official website.\n\nSection 3: Sets effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMunicipal website provides residents with basic information on elected\nofficials, services, local laws and municipal documents. This bill would\ncreate an unprecedented level of transparency by requiring that all\nlocal governments maintain a website where residents can access up to\ndate information.\n\nA 2014 report by the Empire Center highlighted the poor quality of\nmunicipal websites many of which lacked basic information. The report\nfound that less than 20t of local governments received a passing grade\non their website's availability of information and usability including\ntwo municipalities that did not have a website.\n\nThis reports findings are troubling. In 2019, there is no excuse for a\nmunicipality not to have a website that is updated regularly and\nprovides citizens with basic information about the operations of their\nlocal government.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nSenate\n\n2020 - S4908, Referred to Local Government\n\n2021-2022- S1815, Passed Senate\n\nAssembly\n\n2015-16- A5927, Referred to Local Governments\n\n2017-2018 - A1513, Referred to Local Governments\n\n2020 - A3319, Referred to Local Governments\n\n2021-2022- A1166, Referred to Local Governments\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred twentieth day after it\nshall have become law.\n",
